# Markup

Write ordinary HTML in a component body. This button posts to a Rocci
action and keeps a stable `id` for later morphs:

```rocci
<button id="save" class="primary" disabled data-on:click=@post("/actions/save")>
    Save
</button>
```

Unquoted `@post(...)` is a Rocci action: the compiler knows the path.
A quoted `"@post('/x')"` is opaque Datastar JS. `class={expr}` is a Roc
expression. Valueless `disabled` is a boolean attribute.

| You write | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `<div>…</div>` | Intrinsic HTML |
| `<Hello />` | Local component |
| `<>…</>` | Fragment (no wrapper node) |
| `<br>` / `<img />` | Void HTML |

Unknown PascalCase tags still lower to calls; Roc reports a missing
function later. Dynamic tag names and prop spreading are not supported.

Preserve accessible names: `alt` on images, visible labels on buttons,
and stable `id`s on regions you will morph. Datastar morphs by `id`; a
fragment without a stable target is a no-op.

## Interpolation

Text `{expr}` must be `Str` (`{count.to_str()}`). A body parameter already
typed `Html` is inserted as-is. Markup inside `{…}` is rejected; use
`@if` or a named render function. `@@` is a literal `@`.

## CSS

File-level `@css` styles document chrome (`body`, not `html`). Component
`@css` is scoped: lowering stamps `data-rocci-css` and wraps rules in CSS
`@scope`. Authors keep writing `class="card"`. Do not ride a sibling
`<style>` on an SSE patch.

[Styling.rocci](/examples/styling/source/Styling-rocci/) shows file-level
chrome and a component-scoped `.greeting`.

Exact forms: [Tags](/docs/reference/language/tags/),
[Attributes](/docs/reference/language/attributes/),
[CSS](/docs/reference/language/css/).

